.TH GIT-SHOOK 1 "2026-09-05" "git-shook 0.1.0" "Git Extensions"
.SH NAME
git-shook \- run several tools at one git hook event
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git shook install
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B git shook list
.br
.B git shook doctor
.RB [ \-\-only
.IR name ]
.br
.B git shook uninstall
.br
.B git shook version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Git runs one program per hook event, so a tool that installs a hook replaces
whatever was there, with nothing printed and nothing to notice.
.B git-shook
puts one dispatcher at the event and gives every tool a place to register beside
the others.
.PP
A project declares what it wants run in a
.IR shook.toml ,
committed, anywhere in the tree.
.B git shook install
reads every tracked manifest, shows the argument list each one would run, and
registers them once the operator agrees.
.PP
Nothing is installed until
.B install
is run, and manifests are read only then, never when a hook fires. A manifest
arriving through a merge therefore does nothing until somebody looks at it.
.PP
A declaration that only one clone has goes in
.I <git-dir>/shook/manifests/<name>.toml
in the same format. It is read beside the tracked manifests, runs from the
repository root, and replaces a tracked manifest of the same name as a whole.
.B git shook uninstall
leaves that directory in place.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install " [\-\-yes] [\-\-only name]..."
Read every tracked manifest, print what each would run, and register it. With
.B \-\-yes
it does not ask, which is what a script wants and what a person should not use
the first time. With
.BI \-\-only " name"
(repeatable) only the manifests of that name are registered and pruned; every
other manifest stays as it was, registered or not, so a tool registering its own
declaration does not also say yes to one that arrived through a merge.
.TP
.B list
What is registered, per event, in the order it runs.
.TP
.B doctor
.RB [ \-\-only
.IR name ]
Whether what the manifests declare and what is registered still agree, entry by
entry, with each dispatcher this git\-shook's and nothing an older one
left beside them.
Exits non\-zero when they do not.
.B \-\-only
checks the manifests of that name and no other, as
.B install \-\-only
registers them, so a tool checks its own registration without failing on
another's.
A name no manifest has is named and exits non\-zero, and a flag doctor does not
take is refused.
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove the registry, clear
.IR core.hooksPath ,
and put back whatever hook was at the event before.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH THE MANIFEST
.PP
.EX
name = "ante"

[hooks.pre\-commit]
run = ["ante", "fix"]
.EE
.PP
.I run
is an argument list rather than a command line. Nothing goes through a shell, so
there is no quoting to get wrong and no way for an argument to become a second
command. A hook needing a pipe or a redirect ships a script and names it, which
puts the thing that actually runs in a file somebody can read in a diff.
.PP
Each hook runs with its own manifest's directory as the working directory.
.I name
decides the filename in the registry and therefore the order, which is sorted
under the C collation; left out, the directory's name is used. A name carrying a
path separator is refused, as is an event git does not have, and two manifests
claiming one name at one event.
.PP
Stdin is read only at the events git feeds it to:
.BR pre\-push ,
.BR pre\-receive ,
.BR post\-receive ,
.B post\-rewrite
and
.BR reference\-transaction .
There every entry gets its own copy. Elsewhere entries get nothing on stdin and
the caller's input is left alone, except
.BR proc\-receive ,
whose protocol runs both ways and is passed through as it is.
.PP
At
.B fsmonitor\-watchman
stdout is the reply to git, so one entry answers and the others run with their
output discarded. An entry declares it with
.B answers = true
in its section, and a second one is refused. Without one, a hook that was
already at the event answers, and without that the dispatcher replies that
every path may have changed.
.I answers
anywhere else is refused. To use it, set
.I core.fsmonitor
to the absolute path of the dispatcher, which in a linked worktree is under the
common git directory;
.B uninstall
unsets it again when it names the dispatcher, and
.B doctor
reports one naming a dispatcher that is gone.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I shook.toml
A manifest. Any number of them, anywhere in the tree, and each must be tracked
by git to be discovered.
.TP
.I .git/shook/hooks/
Where
.I core.hooksPath
points. One dispatcher per event. Inside the git directory, so it is neither
committed nor something anybody has to ignore.
.TP
.I .git/shook/entries/<event>/<name>
One registered entry. The first line is the working directory relative to the
repository root, and every line after it is one argument.
.TP
.I .git/shook/answers/<event>
The name of the entry that answers the event's protocol on stdout.
.TP
.I .git/shook/kept/<event>
Whatever hook was at the event before, still running as the first entry. A hook
that does nothing but run what a manifest declares at that same event is not
kept, since it is the entrypoint a tool wrote before it moved onto a manifest
and keeping it would run that tool twice.
.TP
.I .git/shook/declined/<event>
A copy of a hook left in place for that reason. A tool that chains to the
repository's own hook after its checks skips the file while it equals this copy.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR doctor ,
everything declared is registered.
.TP
.B 1
A manifest was refused, an operation failed, or
.B doctor
found a disagreement.
.SH SECURITY
Git will not read a hooks path out of the repository, deliberately: if it did,
cloning a repository would execute whatever that repository said to execute. The
activation therefore lives in
.I .git/config
and cannot be committed, and running
.B git shook install
is the consent a committed setting could never express.
.PP
For the same reason manifests are read during
.B install
and never when a hook fires.
.SH LIMITATIONS
An argument containing a newline cannot be represented, since an entry file is
one argument per line, and such a manifest is refused rather than silently
turned into two arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR githooks (5),
.BR git-config (1)
.SH AUTHOR
orgrinrt <orgrinrt@ikiuni.dev>
